    "summary": "High‑energy September ’72 with monster Other One jam & stellar set",
    "blurb": "The show delivers standout renditions of Jack Straw, Friend Of The Devil and a soaring Stella Blue. A 35‑minute The Other One marathon defines the second set, while Truckin' → The Other One flows with relentless drive. Overall performance is tight and inspired despite some mix quirks.",
    "review": "September 9, 1972 at the Hollywood Palladium finds the Dead in top form, riding a wave of high‑octane improvisation that still feels fresh today. Jerry’s guitar shines on Stella Blue and the expansive Other One excursion, while his vocal tone remains clear even through the tape’s occasional drop‑outs. Phil anchors every jam with bass that cuts through the mud of the fairer mixes, providing a solid backbone for the band's daring segues. Bob adds playful lyrical twists on Me And My Uncle and drops a cheeky Truckin’ tease before diving into the marathon Other One. Keith’s piano work supplies tasteful color and smooth transitions, especially noticeable in the soundboard recordings that capture his ad‑libs with crispness. Bill (Billy) keeps the rhythmic engine humming, driving the long Truckin' → Other One stretch with steady, propulsive drumming. Whether you listen to the clean Miller soundboard or an energetic audience tape, the show’s chemistry and daring improvisations make it a must‑hear snapshot of the Dead’s early‑’70s evolution.",
